						<div class='article-twenty-twenty-style-content mb-5 '><p>It’s hard, in the era of pig,<br />
to remember what it felt like.<br />
Topanga Boulevard winds up<br />
Topanga Canyon<br />
from Topanga Beach.<br />
Charles Manson saw a spaceship<br />
land here while Kenneth Anger<br />
was exorcizing the Pentagon.<br />
The only problem with California<br />
is the fire thing.<br />
And the cars, the mouthwash,<br />
the aerospace industry,<br />
the tech sector, the think tanks,<br />
the subdivisions, the landfills.<br />
The real estate, the scene.<br />
I learned punk rock in Colorado,<br />
<em>Straight Outta Compton</em><br />
on a janky tape deck<br />
down 285 outside Fairplay.<br />
Grassland basin, bison<br />
nosing turf along the highway.<br />
In Joshua Tree I’m trying<br />
to find a desert tortoise<br />
in the road so I can follow<br />
the Fish and Wildlife Service<br />
instructions for helping<br />
a tortoise cross the road<br />
so they don’t get creamed.<br />
Quietly approach the tortoise<br />
is step one.<br />
Last step is wash your hands.<br />
I feel that all my life<br />
without knowing it<br />
I’ve been perfecting<br />
my quiet tortoise approach.<br />
But I’d like to know how<br />
I’m supposed to wash my hands<br />
in the desert.<br />
I like step two, note the direction<br />
the tortoise was heading.<br />
If only things were that simple.<br />
I decide it makes more sense<br />
to note the direction<br />
before I quietly approach.<br />
But I don’t see any tortoises.<br />
I see: rocks, dirt, Joshua trees,<br />
various birds, one jackrabbit.<br />
I only see the jackrabbit<br />
for a second.<br />
And the sky. I see the sky.<br />
I don’t know where I’m heading,<br />
but I note the direction<br />
in which some clouds<br />
are crossing the road.<br />
I carefully pick up a cloud<br />
holding it upright<br />
in its normal walking position.<br />
I carry it carefully across the road<br />
no more than a few hundred yards<br />
into the desert<br />
and place it in the shade.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>On a dark desert highway<br />
heading in the direction<br />
of Twentynine Palms<br />
I play the song.<br />
Anton LaVey was rumored<br />
to be visible in the high window<br />
on the inner gatefold<br />
just as he was rumored<br />
to play the hairy devil<br />
in <em>Rosemary’s Baby</em>.<br />
Susan Atkins danced topless<br />
in LaVey’s Witches’ Workshop<br />
before murdering<br />
Polanski’s wife, Sharon Tate,<br />
who was photographed<br />
at Joshua Tree in 1968.<br />
Still those voices are calling<br />
from far away. The desert<br />
is full of old metal<br />
shafts jabbing up<br />
from the void.<br />
Mötley Crüe learned punk rock in Hollywood.<br />
Of course they covered ‘Helter Skelter’.<br />
This was long after Charles Manson<br />
stole Doris Day’s green telescope.<br />
When Nikki Sixx was working on<br />
‘Shout with the Devil’<br />
an Elektra A&amp;R man witnessed<br />
a knife and fork rise from the table<br />
and stick in the ceiling.<br />
He recommended Sixx change<br />
the title to ‘Shout <em>at</em> the Devil’<br />
forthwith. It’s a perfect song,<br />
and in eighth grade<br />
when two metalheads came to my house<br />
looking to beat me up<br />
for insulting their musical taste,<br />
as I had in fact done,<br />
I invited them in,<br />
showed them my metal tapes,<br />
and we all grooved for a while<br />
to Def Leppard and the Crüe,<br />
though they advised me to get serious<br />
and check out Slayer and Iron Maiden,<br />
advice which has served me well<br />
down to the present day.<br />
I don’t believe in spontaneous<br />
demonic levitation of flatware.<br />
As Slayer’s drummer put it,<br />
‘I don’t care about all these things.<br />
I play drums. That’s it.’<br />
Image is everything, as Andre Agassi<br />
and Baudrillard understood.<br />
I just wanna go real hard.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>I’m in Joshua Tree reading an article<br />
called ‘People Keep Finding<br />
Bodies in Joshua Tree’.<br />
The author claims that ‘bodies<br />
don’t bury themselves’,<br />
but the late ’90s taught me<br />
otherwise. I resolve to find<br />
and quietly approach no more<br />
than one body. Let the dead<br />
raise the dead. I’ll put my hand<br />
to the plough and look back.<br />
I like John 8, where they bring<br />
the adulteress to Jesus. They’re like,<br />
the law says we should stone her,<br />
and he just stoops down<br />
and writes in the dirt with his finger.<br />
I assume he was writing<br />
lyrics from his favorite bands.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>Some assholes cut down<br />
a bunch of Joshua trees.<br />
I hate U2, but I saw them<br />
at the Garden for free,<br />
because I’d never been<br />
to the Garden. I posted<br />
about how bored I was,<br />
and to entertain me, Joshua,<br />
no relation to the trees,<br />
posted a long thing<br />
about how he quit <em>Spin</em>,<br />
which blew up online,<br />
and now Joshua’s gone, too.<br />
The last song he turned me on to<br />
says ‘If you’re resourceful<br />
anything can be the right weapon,’<br />
which reminds me of Ani’s<br />
‘Every tool is a weapon<br />
if you hold it right.’</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>You can see why all the freaks<br />
end up in the desert.<br />
Saint Anthony, Manson,<br />
Brigham Young. Hard-luck<br />
scrabblers out for gore<br />
or God or both. Rock<br />
pictograph, coyote scrub,<br />
brimstone cowboys.<br />
Strike-slip along the Eureka.<br />
Adrianne Lenker’s out here<br />
in a trailer. She just wants to go<br />
real soft. Athanasius says the devil<br />
threw a silver plate in Anthony’s path<br />
when he lit out for the desert.<br />
But Anthony had his number:<br />
‘May your silver plate go to hell with you.’<br />
These rocks were star-born.<br />
Caught in the devil’s bargain.<br />
I’ve got to get back to the Garden<br />
to see the Knicks play basketball<br />
as the Sugarhill Gang put it in ’79.<br />
The Garden is the opposite of the desert.<br />
There are no numbers in the rafters<br />
and no rafters. No emo teens<br />
along 8th Ave. Neither snow<br />
nor rain nor gloom of night.<br />
Just the greatest hits<br />
of the Precambrian Supereon<br />
rolling on like REO Speedwagon.<br />
I wrote the previous line for the sound<br />
but as an afterthought<br />
did a search for the band<br />
and guess where the album cover<br />
with the tuna fish was shot.<br />
People keep finding bodies<br />
in Joshua Tree.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>Gram Parsons saw spaceships<br />
out here. The internet says<br />
‘Joshua Tree park guides<br />
are given the option<br />
to tell the story<br />
of Parsons’ cremation<br />
during tours.’<br />
It should be <em>Parsons’s</em>.<br />
My grandparents had a sign in their barn<br />
that said <em>The Robbins</em>,<br />
which drove me crazy as a teen.<br />
It’s in my kitchen now.<br />
Beneath the solecism<br />
is a fairly good painting<br />
of my grandfather’s horse,<br />
Shane, named for <em>Shane</em>.<br />
Frosty, my grandmother’s horse,<br />
was kind of a dick<br />
and once bit her ‘on the tit’<br />
as she liked to tell visitors.<br />
Those summers outside<br />
Rose Hill, Kansas, dirt roads<br />
for light years – the Delines<br />
have a song that says<br />
‘It was the only time<br />
I ever felt right<br />
about anything.’<br />
And now, visions of bodies<br />
being burned. Every star<br />
is a death star in the end.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>Who’s gonna pay for all this?<br />
We used to break into the high school,<br />
drink in the classrooms, write<br />
stupid shit on the blackboards.<br />
The Jeep got stuck in the bush.<br />
This was before classrooms<br />
were killing fields, before<br />
footage and stills and drills.<br />
Before they made a desert<br />
and called it peace.<br />
Tacitus says ‘the earliest humans,<br />
having no perverse desires,<br />
lived without fault and crime,<br />
without punishment and coercion’.<br />
You shouldn’t sit in an exit row<br />
if you’re unwilling or unable<br />
to help, but actually every row<br />
is an exit row, and no one knows<br />
whether they can help<br />
until they’re called on to do so.<br />
So no one should be punished,<br />
ever, for anything. I don’t know<br />
much, but I know that.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>I hardly ever notice birds,<br />
then one catches my eye –<br />
that ladder-backed woodpecker<br />
on the Joshua tree that looks like<br />
a giant asterisk had babies,<br />
for instance – and birds are all<br />
I think about for the next hour.<br />
In this respect birds are like<br />
other people. And other people<br />
are like the desert tortoise,<br />
floating in the sky, changing<br />
shape, blotting out the sun.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>Bury the dead, they say.<br />
Have faith in the war.<br />
A desert is a warm gun,<br />
and any rock is the first tool.<br />
It’s not killing that’s wrong<br />
but choosing the wrong target.<br />
Lisa told me the bully<br />
I fantasized about taking apart<br />
fell out of a tree, high,<br />
and died, decades ago.<br />
So I found another body,<br />
without knowing it,<br />
and it buried itself.<br />
And when we were kids<br />
we walked all over town<br />
by ourselves. Our parents<br />
never had a clue where we were.<br />
It’s not that they trusted the world<br />
but they consented to live in it.<br />
And my brain is still a map<br />
of that town, and I still name<br />
tornadoes after metal girls<br />
I let copy my homework.<br />
And I learned then that loss<br />
is a thread and the soul<br />
is a needle.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>The songs know. Yesterday<br />
I got so old. I want to hurt<br />
the hand inside me. I hate<br />
children, but now, past fifty,<br />
I get why people have them.<br />
They’re an anti-loneliness drug.<br />
You can take them to the desert<br />
and they’ll scare away<br />
the interesting birds<br />
but you’ll have a grand old time<br />
and take pictures<br />
they’ll look at when they’re old<br />
and lonely and wondering<br />
what the point of anything is.<br />
Maybe I should put it plainly<br />
for once. I want the feeling back.<br />
Making out with a witch<br />
in a coffee truck. Four hundred<br />
cigarettes on Mary’s floor.<br />
My old Ford, a fire road.<br />
The little things I didn’t know<br />
were everything. But it sounds<br />
stupid when I put it like that.<br />
Midlife baby mourns lost youth.<br />
At least I’m not boring<br />
someone in some dark café.<br />
<span lang="en-US">The wise course would be acceptance.<br />
</span><span lang="en-US">But I hate wisdom.</span></p>
